too, and the hot turkey sandwich is nigh-perfect. I reccomend eating here in a big group, which this place seems made for. Good gumbo."},{"business_id":"lyJGznk9M6BzCqzmGiWhag","review_id":"kulYnfcw-LBinbeLfYQ0Rg","date":"2008-01-27T01:33:37","stars":4,"text_representativeness":0.930159330368042,"text_excerpt":"Damn good local food served by locals for locals. Delicious and amazingly fresh fried seafood is always a draw. I wasn't a huge fan of their po-boys, but I'm insanely picky in that regard. The pepper rings (think onion rings with bell peppers) are an all-time favorite. Can't beat this place with a stick. Cheap too. Cash only."},{"business_id":"lyJGznk9M6BzCqzmGiWhag","review_id":"50Ga7RoUiyAUz9n8rAqNbw","date":"2006-12-02T20:45:06","stars":4,"text_representativeness":0.9299533367156982,"text_excerpt":"This is deff one of my fave places in New Orleans to go. It's def a whole in the wall so don't go expecting fine dining, but the food is awesome. Me and my friends used to go for crawfish, and they also have pretty good gumbo. It's a great place to go if you're new to town and want to try out local food, and I gaurantee you'll go back. They (of course) have lots of abita on tap which I love."}],"visual":[]},"notebook_case":null}
